{{- if has_odata -}}
# OData: paginated list endpoints

Package: `Pervaxis.Genesis.OData` (already referenced and registered in this service).

## What the package does

- Reads `$filter`, `$orderby`, `$select`, `$expand`, `$top`, `$skip` and `$count` from the request.
- **Validates** them against limits (max page size, filter depth, complexity score) and returns an RFC 7807 `400` if a request breaks a limit.
- Stores the parsed values on the request so your code can read them.
- Provides the `PageResult<T>` response envelope (`value`, `@odata.count`, `@odata.nextLink`).

## What the package does NOT do

It does **not** turn `$filter` or `$orderby` into database queries. Your service decides what to apply. In the example below only `$top`, `$skip` and `$count` are applied. If you need filtering or sorting, map each allowed field to an explicit LINQ expression yourself. Never build queries from the raw `$filter` string.

## Settings

Limits are read from the `Genesis:OData` configuration section. All values are optional. The defaults are shown.

```json
{
  "Genesis": {
    "OData": {
      "MaxTop": 100,
      "DefaultPageSize": 20,
      "MaxExpandDepth": 2,
      "MaxFilterDepth": 3,
      "MaxOrderByProperties": 3,
      "EnableCount": true,
      "MaxQueryComplexityScore": 50
    }
  }
}
```

## Controller

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.OData.Filters;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.OData.Options;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.OData.Results;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.OData.Services;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Products.Controllers;

[ApiController]
[Route("api/v1/products")]
public sealed class ProductsController(IProductService service) : ControllerBase
{
    // Per-endpoint limits override the global settings.
    [HttpGet]
    [ODataQueryable(
        MaxTop = 50,
        AllowedQueryOptions = ODataQueryOptions.Top | ODataQueryOptions.Skip | ODataQueryOptions.Count)]
    [ProducesResponseType(typeof(PageResult<ProductResponse>), StatusCodes.Status200OK)]
    public async Task<ActionResult<PageResult<ProductResponse>>> ListAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        // The OData filter has already validated the query and stored the parsed values here.
        var query = (ODataQueryContext)HttpContext.Items["Genesis.OData.QueryContext"]!;

        var page = await service.ListAsync(query.Skip, query.EffectiveTop, query.CountRequested, cancellationToken);
        return Ok(page);
    }
}
```

## Service

```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Pervaxis.Genesis.OData.Results;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Products.Services;

public sealed class ProductService(ProductDbContext dbContext) : IProductService
{
    public async Task<PageResult<ProductResponse>> ListAsync(
        int skip, int top, bool includeCount, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        // Always order before Skip/Take so pages are stable.
        var query = dbContext.Products.AsNoTracking().OrderBy(p => p.Id);

        long? total = includeCount ? await query.LongCountAsync(cancellationToken) : null;

        var items = await query
            .Skip(skip)
            .Take(top)
            .Select(p => new ProductResponse { Id = p.Id, Name = p.Name })
            .ToListAsync(cancellationToken);

        return new PageResult<ProductResponse> { Value = items, Count = total };
    }
}
```

## Try it

```
GET /api/v1/products?$top=10&$skip=20&$count=true
```

Response shape:

```json
{
  "value": [ { "id": "1", "name": "Widget" } ],
  "@odata.count": 134
}
```

## Notes

- `ProductResponse` must be a class or record (the envelope requires a reference type).
- Use `PageResult<T>` for OData-style endpoints. Use `Common/PagedResult<T>` (page and pageSize style) for plain REST list endpoints. Do not mix them on one endpoint.
- Per-entity property rules can be registered through `EntityConfigurationRegistry`. See the OData package README.
